    {
      "id": "med-22-05",
      "type": "mcq",
      "theme": "analyse",
      "category": "Suites",
      "difficulty": "concours",
      "prompt": "Soit $(u_n)$ définie par $u_0 = 1$ et $u_{n+1} = \\frac{u_n}{1+u_n}$. Alors $u_n$ vaut :",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$\\frac{1}{n+1}$",
          "isCorrect": true
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$\\frac{1}{2^n}$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$\\frac{n}{n+1}$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$\\frac{1}{n}$",
          "isCorrect": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Posons $v_n = \\frac{1}{u_n}$. Alors $v_{n+1} = \\frac{1+u_n}{u_n} = v_n + 1$. Suite arithmétique : $v_n = v_0 + n = 1+n$. Donc $u_n = \\frac{1}{n+1}$."
    },
